WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:03.169
-Our first guest tonight
is a United States senator

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:05.171
from Vermont.
His best selling book,

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.707
"It's Okay to Be Angry About
Capitalism," is out in paperback

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.542
February 20th
with a new afterword.

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:10.810
Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:13.279
Senator Bernie Sanders,
everyone.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.581
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:22.121 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:28.627 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:30.730
-Welcome back, Senator.

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:32.165
-Good to be with you, Seth.

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:33.933
-First of all,
I want to congratulate you.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.369
You were nominated for a Grammy
for Best Audiobook.

00:00:36.435 --> 00:00:38.604
-Lost again.
-Lost again.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:39.872
You lost to Michelle Obama.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.374
Everything's so political
these days.

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:42.875
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:46.612
-How quickly did you RSVP
"no" to going to the Grammys?

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:49.015
-Pretty quickly.
-Yeah.

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:51.584
-Despite strong
family opposition.

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.152
-They wanted you to go.
-Grandchildren wanted.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.189
-Everybody wanted to be a plus
one to grandpa at the Grammys.

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:58.257
Now, real quick, when
you read your audiobook --

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:00.426
because obviously, this
is your book and you read it --

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:01.927
this is a book
about being angry.

00:01:01.994 --> 00:01:05.198
Did the people who were editing
you in the booth ever say,

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:06.665
like, "Maybe can
this next read --

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:08.567
could you be
a little less angry?"

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Was I angry for 16 straight
hours?

00:01:10.936 --> 00:01:13.573
Is that your question?
-I guess -- Is it possible?

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.541
-No. I had some
boring statistics

00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:16.976
that I had to read,
you know.

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:18.878
-That's very smart to put
in the statistics.

00:01:18.944 --> 00:01:21.547
-Three people own more wealth
than the bottom half,

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:22.648
you know.
Yeah.

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.383
-A few things to bring it down.
-Right.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.320
-Some people that I imagine
maybe are not thrilled

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:30.890
to get a call from you would be
pharmaceutical executives,

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:33.159
and you have managed
to wrangle them in

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:36.195
for hearings this week
in Washington, DC.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:37.964
This is your office doing this.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.565
This is you reaching out
to have these hearings.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.000
What happens when they hear,

00:01:41.066 --> 00:01:43.402
you know,
"Bernie Sanders on Line One?"

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:47.039
-They are very unavailable.

00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:48.674
-Yeah.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:52.444
-Look, our healthcare system is,

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:56.249
I think most people know,
is dysfunctional.

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:59.218
We pay twice as much
per capita for health care

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.087
as any other people.

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:04.256
We don't have enough doctors,
nurses, dentists.

00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:07.060
Our life expectancy
is not only lower

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:10.029
than other major countries,
it's getting worse.

00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:12.732
And if you're working class
or poor, the gap between

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:15.634
rich and poor in this country
is over ten years.

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:17.070
So the system is failing.

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:20.339
And part of that failure
is the incredible greed

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.441
of the pharmaceutical industry.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:25.177
So the questions that we're
gonna ask these top executives,

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:26.712
very simple --

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:30.516
why in God's name are you
charging us, in some cases,

00:02:30.582 --> 00:02:34.854
ten times more for the
same bloody drug that you sell

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.723
people -- sell to people
in Canada or in Europe?

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.425
Ten times more.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:42.328
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:44.396 --> 00:02:49.835
And Seth, what happens is --
And this is really crazy stuff.

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:52.371
One out of four Americans
go to the pharmacist -- doctor

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.573
writes them a prescription,
go to the pharmacist.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.375
They can't afford
to fill their drugs --

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.177
to fill their prescription.

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.613
So what happens to those people?
They end up getting sicker.

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:02.014
Maybe they end up
in the emergency room.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:04.884
Maybe they end up in
the hospital, at great expense

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:06.852
to the system --
financial expense --

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:10.689
not to mention human suffering
and sometimes death.

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:15.828
So what you got is, like many
other entities in Washington,

00:03:15.894 --> 00:03:17.363
you have
a pharmaceutical industry

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:18.797
which has unlimited
amounts of money.

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:21.868
They made $110 billion
in profits last year.

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:25.271
They have 1,800 well-paid
lobbyists,

00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:27.739
former heads of
the Democratic Republican Party.

00:03:27.806 --> 00:03:31.077
They spend zillions of dollars
on campaign contributions.

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:32.611
But I think what
the American people,

00:03:32.678 --> 00:03:34.947
whether they're Republican,
Democrat, or independent,

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:38.818
want us to do is say,
stop the bloody greed,

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:42.188
substantially lower prescription
drug prices in America.

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.757
-Now, this is something
of course --

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:46.359
we've talked about this
in the past.

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:49.228
I feel almost every Democratic
administration,

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:50.830
politicians promises
to do this.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.932
Is there anything in the first
three years

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.834
of the Biden administration that
we should be optimistic about?

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:57.703
-Yeah, we made some progress.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.006
And of course, the industry
is going a little bit nuts.

00:04:01.073 --> 00:04:02.675
The reason that we pay
so much more

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:04.610
than other countries
is all of these countries,

00:04:04.676 --> 00:04:08.013
by the way, have national
health care programs

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.484
which guarantee health care
to all people as a human right.

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:15.654
Makes sense to me.
All right. We don't.

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:18.257
But when you have
a national health care system,

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:19.726
you sit down
with the drug companies

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.026
and you say, "You want
to sell your drugs

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:23.662
into the system?
Good. Let's negotiate a price."

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:25.932
That's reasonable.
That's intelligence.

00:04:25.998 --> 00:04:29.735
We have never done that.
So up until a year ago,

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:31.937
the drug companies could charge
any price they want.

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:33.739
They can come up
with most weird reason

00:04:33.805 --> 00:04:35.807
double, triple,
quadruple prices.

00:04:35.874 --> 00:04:38.911
What we did in a bill called
the Inflation Reduction Act

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:44.417
is to begin -- begin the process
of negotiating prescription

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:49.288
drug prices for Medicare with
the pharmaceutical industry.

00:04:49.354 --> 00:04:50.823
It's a small step forward,

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:54.259
but it is very, very important.
We got to expand that greatly.

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:58.431
The other thing we've done
is for older people on Medicare.

00:04:58.497 --> 00:05:02.468
They won't pay more than
$35 a month for insulin

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:05.437
if they have diabetes.
That's a step forward as well.

00:05:05.504 --> 00:05:06.672
-That's a step
in the right direction.

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:09.942
It's very exciting.
I want to ask,

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:13.312
we talked about on the show
because it was

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.380
so out of the ordinary
to all of us.

00:05:15.447 --> 00:05:17.649
It was a labor
hearing in November

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:19.017
you were presiding over,

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:21.054
and one of your
Republican colleagues,

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.591 size:75% position:63%
a senator from Oklahoma,
almost came to blows

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.326
with the head of -- one
of the heads of --

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.662
a labor leader here
in the United States.

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:31.997
Let's show the clip first,
and then I'd like to ask you

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.632
what was going through your mind
when this happened.

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:35.234
Do you want to do it now?

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.336
-I'd love to do it right now.
-Well, stand your butt up, then.

00:05:37.402 --> 00:05:39.171
-You stand your butt up.
-Oh! Hold on -- Hold on --

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:40.406
Stop it.

00:05:40.472 --> 00:05:41.741
-Is that your solution
to every problem?

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:43.943
-No, no. Sit down, sit down.
-Look at you.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.446
-You know, you're
a United States senator.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:47.847
-Okay.
-Sit down, please.

00:05:47.913 --> 00:05:49.348
-All right.
-Can I respond?

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.683
-Hold it, hold.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.353
-If we can't -- no.
I have the mic. I'm sorry.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:55.787
-This is --
-Hold it.

00:05:55.854 --> 00:05:56.756
You'll have your time.

00:05:56.822 --> 00:05:58.858
-Can I respond?
-No, you can't.

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:01.860
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.927 --> 00:06:02.761
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:02.828 --> 00:06:05.531
-I like -- Even --

00:06:05.597 --> 00:06:08.000
Even if we couldn't have
heard you, I knew this was you.

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.435
-Like ET's finger, wasn't it?

00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:11.703
-Your hand already looks like
a sock puppet

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.473
from a "Sesame Street."

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.241
Now, what was -- what was going
through your head when --

00:06:16.308 --> 00:06:17.710
I mean, by the way, nice job.

00:06:17.777 --> 00:06:20.079
You kept it together there.
It did not come to blows.

00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:21.580
That must have felt like
a personal victory.

00:06:21.647 --> 00:06:24.383
-This is the state of
the United States Senate.

00:06:24.449 --> 00:06:26.752
We've prevented a fistfight
in a committee hearing.

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:29.454
Wow. What next?

00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:32.358
-Well, what was going through
your mind when, you know?

00:06:32.425 --> 00:06:34.159
-Yeah, it's crazy stuff.

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:36.963
And it's -- what is
particularly upsetting,

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:39.865
Seth, you know what the purpose
of that hearing

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:42.835
was that nobody knew about
after all of that?

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:44.970
So let me tell you,
in case you didn't know.

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:47.406
We were talking
about corporate greed

00:06:47.472 --> 00:06:50.609
and the very positive role
that unions are playing

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:53.211
in standing up
for workers in America.

00:06:53.278 --> 00:06:54.446
Now, nobody got to learn

00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:56.115
about that
because that was what happened.

00:06:56.181 --> 00:06:58.216
But, you know, that's what
we were trying to do.

00:06:58.283 --> 00:07:00.085
-I feel like your book,

00:07:00.152 --> 00:07:01.687
"It's Okay to be Angry
About Capitalism,"

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:04.657
the photo that you chose
for the front makes it look like

00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:06.725
you're a guy who's not going
to let any nonsense

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:10.429
happen when he's in charge.
That's, like, very much like,

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:13.799
"You don't want me to roll
this shirt up."

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:16.368
Seems to be the energy
of this one.

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:20.273
Were you happy
when you picked this photo?

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:21.873
-Was I happy?
-Were you?

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:23.876
I mean, I'm going to stop
saying "were you happy?"

00:07:23.942 --> 00:07:26.378
when to you, Bernie?
[ Laughs ]

00:07:26.445 --> 00:07:28.915
-I look determined.
That's a good look.

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:30.716
-Yeah, yeah. Determined.
You look very determined.

00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:33.218
I have a lot more
to ask you about.

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:36.021
Your determination will be more
with Senator Sanders

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:37.123
right after this.

